      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To find \\(\\sin \\frac{11\\pi}{3}\\), we first need to reduce the angle to an equivalent angle within the interval \\([0, 2\\pi)\\). This is done by subtracting multiples of \\(2\\pi\\) (or \\(6\\pi/3\\)) from \\(\\frac{11\\pi}{3}\\) until the result is within the desired range.\n\nFirst, we calculate how many full rotations of \\(2\\pi\\) fit into \\(\\frac{11\\pi}{3}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{11\\pi}{3} - 2\\pi = \\frac{11\\pi}{3} - \\frac{6\\pi}{3} = \\frac{5\\pi}{3}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\frac{5\\pi}{3}\\) is still greater than \\(2\\pi\\) and less than \\(3\\pi\\), it is within the interval \\([0, 2\\pi)\\). Therefore, \\(\\sin \\frac{11\\pi}{3} = \\sin \\frac{5\\pi}{3}\\).\n\nNext, we need to determine the reference angle for \\(\\frac{5\\pi}{3}\\). The angle \\(\\frac{5\\pi}{3}\\) is in the fourth quadrant, and the reference angle is:\n\n\\[\n2\\pi - \\frac{5\\pi}{3} = \\frac{6\\pi}{3} - \\frac{5\\pi}{3} = \\frac{\\pi}{3}\n\\]\n\nIn the fourth quadrant, the sine of an angle is negative, so:\n\n\\[\n\\sin \\frac{5\\pi}{3} = -\\sin \\frac{\\pi}{3}\n\\]\n\nWe know that \\(\\sin \\frac{\\pi}{3} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\), so:\n\n\\[\n\\sin \\frac{5\\pi}{3} = -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the value of \\(\\sin \\frac{11\\pi}{3}\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}}\n\\]",
